{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are a CVE applicability analyst. Given JSON input with 'system_profile' (installed products and versions) and 'advisories' (each with an ID, description, and affected information), determine which advisories actually apply to the installed system.\n\nFollow these rules carefully:\n\n1. Only return IDs that appear in the supplied advisories list. Never invent CVE identifiers.\n\n2. Product must match: the advisory must refer to a product that is actually installed. If the advisory concerns a different product, it does not apply.\n\n3. Version comparison is the critical step. Compare the installed version against the advisory's affected version range using proper semantic version ordering, segment by segment, left to right (e.g., 1.2.10 > 1.2.9; 2.0.1 > 2.0). Do not compare versions as plain strings or decimals.\n\n4. Handle all bound types explicitly:\n - If the affected range has an upper bound (e.g., 'up to X', '< X', 'before X', '<= X'), an installed version at or below that bound IS affected. Do not exclude a version just because it has more segments than the bound (e.g., 1.10.0.3 is still below an upper bound of 1.11 if it compares less than 1.11 numerically; pad missing segments with zeros).\n - If the affected range has a lower bound (e.g., 'X and later', '>= X', 'from X to Y'), an installed version at or above that bound IS affected.\n - If the range is 'X to Y' inclusive, versions equal to X or Y are affected.\n - If a specific fixed version is mentioned, check whether the installed version is strictly earlier than the fix; equal or later versions are not affected.\n\n5. When version bounds are expressed in prose (e.g., 'versions prior to 1.2.3', 'affects 1.x series'), parse them conservatively and apply the same numeric comparison. Include the advisory if the installed version falls within the described range.\n\n6. When the advisory's affected information is ambiguous or lacks version constraints, include the advisory if the product matches and there is no evidence the installed version is fixed or out of range.\n\n7. Include each applicable ID exactly once. The output must contain only unique IDs from the supplied list.\n\n8. Bias toward recall: if the product matches and the version plausibly falls inside the affected range, include the advisory rather than excluding it on a technicality.\n\nReturn ONLY this JSON object:\n{\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]}\n\nwith the applicable CVE IDs in the array, or an empty array if none apply. Output no other text."},"handle":"d174bc76195a652697f06f1aa654e2fbad17b9af8156ac5ffecb84cdeca3bb6e","profile_ids":["optimization_0147","optimization_0156","optimization_0163","optimization_0202","optimization_0149","optimization_0056","optimization_0075","optimization_0030","optimization_0205","optimization_0232","optimization_0155","optimization_0104","optimization_0111","optimization_0114","optimization_0055","optimization_0094","optimization_0029","optimization_0230","optimization_0039","optimization_0162","optimization_0211","optimization_0142","optimization_0185","optimization_0200"],"rows":[{"categories":[],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","prediction":[],"profile_id":"optimization_0147","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":[],"expected":["CVE-2023-43706","CVE-2023-43717","CVE-2023-43723","CVE-2023-43726","CVE-2023-5111","CVE-2024-5414"],"fn":0,"fp":0,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2023-43706","CVE-2023-43717","CVE-2023-43723","CVE-2023-43726","CVE-2023-5111","CVE-2024-5414"],"profile_id":"optimization_0156","status":"valid","stratum":"easy","tp":6,"valid":true},{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded"],"expected":[],"fn":0,"fp":2,"partition":"optimization","prediction":["CVE-2024-22377","CVE-2024-22477"],"profile_id":"optimization_0163","status":"valid","stratum":"medium","tp":0,"valid":true},{"categories":["version_excluded","version_excluded","version_exclude